# null control
rep(r"The null flow of \cref{inh:null-flow} deletes every peer cost. Here it keeps the angular self terms and gives, on the binary family, the decoupled field $\dot x_i=-(1-x_i^2)\operatorname{atanh}x_i$, whose scaled limit is $\dot u=-u$, so $u(t)=e^{-t}u(0)$. Read by the same rows and \cref{open:selector}, its membership history is a function of the initial positions alone: the rows at time $t$ are a Gaussian kernel of bandwidth $e^t$ on $u(0)$. Integrations of \cref{open:limit} and of this null limit from the initial data of the proof, at $A=8$, $64$, and $4096$ with a distant third pair, produce the same three pairs, the same core-parent join with the same exterior, and the same order of events; the pair events move from $t\approx0.589$ to $0.647$, and at $A=4096$ the parent join moves from $t\approx8.214$ to $8.271$. These are integrations of the limit field, not of the exact scaled field at the fixed $h$ of the proof, and they are not part of any statement proved here. What is proved is weaker and one-sided: while $a\ge4$ the internal half-gap contracts along the full field at a rate between $0.977$ and $3.03$ by \cref{open:early-bounds}, against exactly $1$ along the null limit, so the peer forces tighten the selected pairs. Neither interaction necessity (F5) nor the amplification grade of \cref{inh:recursive-formation} is established by \cref{open:routing}.",
    r"""Both nulls of \cref{inh:null-flow} delete every peer cost; the label-preserving null keeps the peer labels and priors, and the renormalized null removes them. On the binary family every self cost vanishes, so the two nulls coincide there and give the decoupled field $\dot x_i=-(1-x_i^2)\operatorname{atanh}x_i$, that is $\operatorname{atanh}x_i(t)=e^{-t}\operatorname{atanh}x_i(0)$, with scaled limit $u(t)=e^{-t}u(0)$. For correlated laws the angular self cost $H_i^q$ is nonzero and the nulls differ: the angular conditionals relax at rate two along the renormalized null and at the agent-dependent rate $1+\beta^0_{ii}$, with the self weight \cref{inh:null-self-row}, along the label-preserving null, so the bit forcing \cref{open:bit-forcing} differs. By the readout convention of \cref{inh:null-flow}, the selector reads along a null the native rows \cref{open:row} computed from the original costs at the null state. On the binary family its membership history is then a function of the initial positions alone: the rows at time $t$ are a Gaussian kernel of bandwidth $e^t$ on $u(0)$. Integrations of \cref{open:limit} and of this null limit from reference configurations of the form used in the proof, at $A=8$, $64$, and $4096$, with the third pair at $R=200$, $2000$, and $4\times10^5$ rather than at the far larger $R$ of the proof, produce the same three pairs, the same core-parent join with the same exterior, and the same order of events; the pair events move from $t\approx0.589$ to $0.647$, and at $A=4096$ the parent join moves from $t\approx8.214$ to $8.271$. These are integrations of the limit field, not of the exact scaled field at the fixed $h$ of the proof, and they are not part of any statement proved here. The near-simultaneous first-stage pair events are merges of disjoint agent sets, so a reordering of them between the full and null flows does not change the partition sequence as compared by \cref{inh:null-flow}.

What is proved is one-sided: while $a\ge4$ and $0<b\le2$ the internal half-gap contracts along the full field at a rate above one, against exactly one along the null limit. Indeed, by \cref{open:core}, $-\dot b/b-1=2c_p+2[g_c(a+b)-g_c(a-b)]/b$ with $g_c$ as above. For $b\ge1/2$, $a-b\ge b$ and the decrease of $re^{-2r^2}$ on $[1/2,\infty)$ give $bw\ge(a-b)v_-$, hence $-\dot b/b-1\ge2w/Z_+>0$ after dropping the positive $v_+$ term. For $b\le1$, $a-b\ge3$, the mean-value bound $|g_c(a+b)-g_c(a-b)|\le2b(1+4(a-b)^2)e^{-2(a-b)^2}$, and $c_p\ge2w/(1+w+2e^{-18})$ with $w\ge e^{-2}$ give $-\dot b/b-1>0.47$. The peer forces therefore tighten the selected pairs. Neither interaction necessity (F5) nor the amplifying grade of \cref{inh:recursive-formation} is established by \cref{open:routing}. A proof of the null comparison would run the explicit null solution through the same windows and crossing estimates by \cref{open:persistence}; it is not carried out here.""")

