# C^1 lemma
rep(r"Smooth dependence of the fine field and its contextual variational equations preserves these bounds on a sufficiently small full neighborhood of independent initial laws; this step uses the $C^r$ chart, since the rotation scores are angular derivatives.",
    r"Smooth dependence of the fine field and its contextual variational equations preserves these bounds on a sufficiently small full neighborhood of independent initial laws. This step uses that the fine field is real analytic on the bounded log-density chart, so that the flow map is twice continuously differentiable with derivatives continuous in the initial point uniformly on the horizon \citep{Dieudonne1960}; that equivariance transports angular derivatives, $\partial_\theta\Phi_t(q)=D\Phi_t(q)\partial_\theta q$; and that the curvature $F=-b\partial_\kappa a/(a+b)^2$, with $a$ and $b$ the belief and model rotation inertias, needs only the jets $\partial_\kappa q$, $\partial_\theta q$, and $\partial_\kappa\partial_\theta q$. The neighborhood is therefore open in the $C^1$ chart, hence in the $C^r$ chart; uniform closeness of log-densities, the topology of \cref{open:open-laws}, does not suffice for this step.")

