rep(r"Against \cref{inh:recursive-formation} the theorem gives (F1) with the $C^r$ topology, (F2) with finite-window persistence, (F3) with the response class declared before selection, and (F4), since each retention is non-injective and a pre-handoff query distinguishes its fibers. It does not establish (F5) (\cref{open:null-control}). The first part of (F6) holds: the parent pair consists of first-stage parents under inherited rows, without reset or retuning. Its counterfactual part is not verified, since the second stage depends on the first only through the partner exclusion of \cref{open:selector} and occurs when the supplied core separation has contracted to order one. The construction is therefore a two-stage selection with exact inheritance in the sense of the standard, with that qualification, and not a recursive formation.",
    r"Against \cref{inh:recursive-formation} the report is as follows. The objective is the reciprocal potential with exact Gibbs rows, and the dynamics is its unit-Fisher flow in the bounded log-density chart, with \cref{prop:dyn-chart-flow} supplying well-posedness. (F1) holds in the full grade: the admitted class is open in the chart, both for uniform convergence of log-densities and in the $C^r$ topology, and for the geometric statement in the $C^1$ topology. (F2) holds for the selector \cref{open:selector} with its two constants fixed at $\varepsilon=1/10$ and $\delta=1/20$, which reads native rows and so satisfies the readout convention, with persistence on finite windows and the finite horizon of \cref{open:formation}; a constant set with nonempty interior is not declared, and over such a set the theorem is not proved. The parent protocol rule assigns to a block the responses \cref{open:response} with native source draws and transmitted bits; the probe rule adds, before a handoff, the protocols of the merged blocks, which before a first join are singleton queries returning individual angles. (F3) holds for these classes by (c). (F4) holds: the newly discarded subgroup gives distinct reachable configurations with the same retained state, separated by a pre-handoff singleton or block query, and the separation is of symmetry type, since the fibers are orbits of the active rotations, and transient, since every angular law relaxes to Haar measure. (F5) is not established against either null. The two nulls coincide on the binary family and differ on correlated laws; integrations of the limit field indicate that both form the same blocks in the same stages, and the frozen-environment control is not computed. (F6a) holds: the parent pair consists of first-stage parents under inherited rows and the same selector, without reset or retuning. (F6b) is not established: no counterfactual class is declared, the second stage depends on the first only through the partner exclusion of \cref{open:selector}, and it occurs when the supplied core separation has contracted to order one. Neither grade is established. The construction is not shown to be a recursive formation, and since no clause is shown to fail, the classification is undetermined, with (F5) and (F6b) open.")

